{
 "_note": "SYNTHETIC -- NOT FETCHED FROM OPENSKY, AND NOT A MEASUREMENT. It accompanies the bundled sample track, which is itself synthetic (README, Limitations 3), so no real flight matches it and no API would return one. It is shaped exactly like an OpenSky /tracks/all response so that `rcta-cli flight` can be exercised end to end with no account and no network. The positions are the track's own smoothed fixes, so the position match passes. The altitude is a straight climb-cruise-descent whose four breakpoints were read off the track's dose-rate crossings: the dose reaches 10 percent of its cruise level at 1645 s and 95 percent at 3226 s, and falls back through the same levels at 7715 s and 8820 s. Nothing else was tuned. RUNNING THE FLIGHT COMMAND ON THIS PAIR REPORTS AN INCONSISTENT PROFILE, AND THAT IS THE CORRECT ANSWER. The climb yields a scale height near 5 km and the descent near 9 km, and no linear altitude profile reconciles them, because the sample track's dose rate was synthesised as a function of TIME rather than of altitude. The climb-versus-descent control detects that. See docs/FLIGHT.md.",
 "icao24": "a1b2c3",
